### Example 2: Claims Service Kafka Message Ordering (Illinois Incident Prevention)

**Scenario**: Kafka Message Ordering Not Tested; Production Data Integrity Issue

**Problem**:
- **Unit Tests**: Mocked `KafkaTemplate` always succeeded immediately; no partition rebalancing testing
- **Production**: Kafka partition rebalancing caused message reordering; claims processed out of sequence
- **Impact**: 15,000 claims processed in wrong order; $450K manual reconciliation; 3-week data cleanup

**Without Integration Tests** (What Happened):

Unit test with mock (passes, but doesn't test Kafka ordering):

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class ClaimsServiceUnitTest {
    @Mock
    private KafkaTemplate<String, ClaimEvent> kafkaTemplate;

    @InjectMocks
    private ClaimsService service;

    @Test
    void shouldPublishClaimEvent() {
        Claim claim = new Claim("CLM-12345", "M12345", BigDecimal.valueOf(150.00));

        when(kafkaTemplate.send(anyString(), any(), any()))
            .thenReturn(CompletableFuture.completedFuture(null));

        service.submitClaim(claim);

        verify(kafkaTemplate).send(eq("claim-submitted"), eq("M12345"), any());
        // TEST PASSES ✓
        // But doesn't test Kafka partition key strategy or message ordering
    }
}
```

**Production**: Kafka partition rebalancing → message reordering → data integrity issue → $450K reconciliation

**With Testcontainers Integration Test** (Prevents Production Incident):

```java
@SpringBootTest
@Testcontainers
class ClaimsServiceKafkaOrderingIntegrationTest {

    @Container
    static KafkaContainer kafka = new KafkaContainer(
        DockerImageName.parse("confluentinc/cp-kafka:7.3.0")
    );

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void kafkaProps(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
        registry.add("spring.kafka.bootstrap-servers", kafka::getBootstrapServers);
    }

    @Autowired
    private ClaimsService claimsService;

    @Test
    void shouldMaintainClaimOrderingForSameMember() {
        // Publish 100 claims for same member (same partition key)
        String memberId = "M12345";
        List<Claim> submittedClaims = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
            BigDecimal amount = BigDecimal.valueOf(i * 10);  // 0, 10, 20, ..., 990
            Claim claim = claimsService.submitClaim(memberId, amount);
            submittedClaims.add(claim);
        }

        // Create consumer to verify ordering
        KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = createConsumer("claim-submitted");
        List<Integer> receivedAmounts = new ArrayList<>();

        // Consume all messages
        await().atMost(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS).untilAsserted(() -> {
            ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofSeconds(1));
            records.forEach(record -> {
                BigDecimal amount = extractAmount(record.value());
                receivedAmounts.add(amount.intValue());
            });
            assertEquals(100, receivedAmounts.size(), "Expected all 100 messages");
        });

        // Verify messages received in correct order (0, 10, 20, ..., 990)
        for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
            assertEquals(i * 10, receivedAmounts.get(i),
                "Message at position " + i + " should have amount " + (i * 10) +
                " but was " + receivedAmounts.get(i));
        }

        // TEST WOULD FAIL if partition key strategy is incorrect
        // (e.g., using claim ID instead of member ID as partition key)
    }
}
```

**Result**:
- **Integration Test Validates Ordering**: All 100 messages received in correct sequence
- **Partition Key Strategy Verified**: Member ID correctly used as partition key
- **Production Deployment**: No message ordering issues; data integrity maintained
- **Incident Prevention ROI**: **$450K cost avoidance**

